  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nToday through next Friday...\n\nNo changes have been made to the forecast, and the overall messaging \nof dangerous heat remains the same. The subtropical ridge will \ncontinue to build, expand, and intensify over the southeast CONUS as \nwe go through the rest of the late week and into the weekend. \nSubsidence beneath the ridge along with increased sunshine will \nlimit rain chances across the south today through Sunday with \nafternoon highs peaking in the upper 90s to lower 100s. Although \nsome of the humidity will be eased due to the strengthened ridge, \nthe combination of heat and humidity will ensure that most days see \npeak heat index reading values in the 105-110 degree range through \nthe weekend. No changes have been made to the heat graphic and a \n\"Elevated\" risk for dangerous heat will continue to be advertised \nfor our entire forecast area into early next week. Likewise, the \nareawide Heat Advisory remains in effect until 8 pm Sunday evening.\n\nDangerous heat will continue across the area heading into early next \nweek. Monday and Tuesday look to be the hottest days of the extended \nperiod with several locations reaching the 100 degree mark. There is \na possibility that a few locations may reach 103 degrees and we may \nsee some new record highs being set, especially if the warmer \nguidance overperforms. \n\nThere is a chance that portions of the \"Elevated\" risk could get \nupgraded to a \"Significant\" risk for dangerous heat where heat \nindices could exceed above 110 degrees as we get closer to Monday. \nAn Extreme Heat Warning may eventually be needed for portions of the \narea for Monday and Tuesday. Given that this focus is still far \nenough out in time, we will hold off on introducing \"Significant\" \nheat risk for now. Additional heat headlines and advisories/warnings \nmay eventually be introduced as we get closer to the mid-week.\n\nRegarding the rain chances, they are mostly non-existent until early \nnext week. At that time, global guidance continues to show a signal \nfor deeper moisture in the westerlies north of the high center to \ncirculate around the high and potentially increase convective \nrainfall chances over eastern portions of the forecast area. Rain \nchances could be further increased by an approaching frontal \nboundary, but any relief from the heat that could result remains \nuncertain at this time. Rain chances will start to increase \nslightly across much of the area (15-25%) heading into Friday. /CR/",
